The glyf table's iov entries point to the input font data (`data + gly_offset`), not the output stream buffer. So the UAF isn't from glyf either.

Let me think about this differently. The UAF in `OTSStream::Write` happens when `data` points into the `ExpandingMemoryStream`'s own buffer. This could happen if a table's `Serialize` function reads data from the output stream. Let me look at the `loca` table or other tables that might reference the output stream.

Actually, let me look at the `cff` table serialization, which might use the output stream's buffer. Or let me check the `head` table which needs to write a checksum.

Actually, let me reconsider the vulnerability. Looking at `ExpandingMemoryStream::WriteRaw`:

```cpp
bool WriteRaw(const void *data, size_t length) {
    if ((off_ + length > length_) ||
        (length > std::numeric_limits<size_t>::max() - off_)) {
      if (length_ == limit_)
        return false;
      size_t new_length = (length_ + 1) * 2;
      ...
      uint8_t* new_buf = new uint8_t[new_length];
      std::memcpy(new_buf, ptr_, length_);
      length_ = new_length;
      delete[] static_cast<uint8_t*>(ptr_);
      ptr_ = new_buf;
      return WriteRaw(data, length);
    }
    std::memcpy(static_cast<char*>(ptr_) + off_, data, length);
    off_ += length;
    return true;
}
```

The UAF occurs when `data` points into the old `ptr_` buffer. After `delete[] ptr_`, the recursive call `WriteRaw(data, length)` reads from freed memory.

When could `data` point into the stream's own buffer? Let me look at the `head` table serialization, which might read from the output stream to compute checksums.

